today we're constantly bombarded with  information that's designed to please  the eye  and to deliver maximum often visual  impact  within a short amount of time  there's million dollar efforts from  large corporations  to grab our attention every way and to  keep it there  when we become immersed into this  colorful information  our other haptic senses of experiencing  the world becomes  desensitized this doesn't only have an  impact on our perception and our  psychology  but it actually also has an indirect  impact on our built environment  with the development of digital  publications and apps like instagram  and pinterest more than ever before we  consume  architecture through the screen rather  than in person  our experience of architecture has been  condensed  into the money shot instead of  physically experiencing the space  this has led to issues of experiential  impoverishment the elements of  architecture are not  visual units or gestalt they're  encounters  confrontations that interact with memory  uh what so what he's trying to say is  that  our experience of architecture can't be  condensed into  visuals and images but what does memory  have to do with all this  in order to understand this we need to  understand this very basic concept in  architecture  the difference between space and place  if two different authors use the word  red  hard or disappeared no one doubts that  they mean approximately  the same thing but in the case of words  such as place  or space whose relationship with  psychological experience is  less direct there exists a far-reaching  uncertainty of interpretations the idea  of space  and place are definitely connected but a  place  compared to a space is all about how we  interact with it in their research paper  neuroscience and architecture  esther sternberg and matthew wilson  found that the same part of the brain is  activated  when navigating around the space and  when  recalling autobiographical events and  this part of the brain  is called hippocampus our memory of  events  may depend upon a strong sense of place  and by extension our sense of place may  be influenced by the integrity of  memories formed there  so when we say haptic sense this is  exactly the thing that's activated by  these  place neurons which respond to a  huge amount of different stimuli  including sight  sound smell and a strong sense of  orientation  within the environment in an interview  with the new york times  peter zomthor explained his process  when i start my first idea of a building  is  the material i believe architecture is  about that  it's not about paper it's not about  forms it's about  space and materials so i visited my very  first peter zomtor building  in 2016 on a trip to europe with my  classmates  and i'd seen so many of his buildings in  photographs and in books  but never in real person and i realized  that none of the photos really did it  justice  because his buildings are really about  the sensory experience  our bus dropped us off at the bottom of  a valley  in a small swiss farming village called  and when we got off the bus we could  literally hear  the silence  from afar the curvature of the walls  almost makes the surfaces  look smooth but up close you see the  depth and color and texture  that kind of speaks to its age  and how many years this church has been  sitting in the elements  the threshold comes out at an angle from  the church  and it's an extension interrupting the  perfect flow of the cylinder  the steps are made of concrete so it's  distinct from the rest of the building  the door itself is clad in vertical  strips of wood and  it's enclosed with a threshold and you  almost get sucked into this intense  verticality the door handle is of course  the last thing you touch from the  outside world and it's your first  encounter with the  inside world  and when you walk in you just walk into  a space that's  filled with warmth and light it's a very  simple interior space just one volume  and a ring of vertical columns with  beams and benches by offsetting the  walls from the columns and the floor  and continuing the columns all the way  to the base  we get a sense of levitating in space  there's no windows just glass panels at  the top  which brings in a flood of light without  distracting us with views of these  beautiful mountains  in one of the most beautiful locations  in the world  my memory of this place is so strong  but strangely enough it's not the views  of the mountains or over the village  but the sound of silence of the gravel  path  of the trickling water the light  and the warm touch of wood  you
 

